WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
 So now we're going to make a database.

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.860
 We're going to use SQLite browser.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.600
 Hopefully you've downloaded it so you can follow along.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.680
 And I've got this handout, this basic database handout

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.800
 that saves you from having to type all these things.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.040
 So bring that up in your web browser.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.920
 And so that gives you all of the commands

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.400
 that I'm going to type now.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.660
 And so you could pull them out of either the web page,

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:28.400
 you can pull them out of the slides,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.800
 or you can pull them out of that.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.800
 So I'm going to bring up the database browser here.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.480
 Database browser.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.040
 Now, the thing that's going to happen,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.640
 you'll see this happen on my desktop,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.240
 I'm going to make a new database,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.800
 and you have to store it somewhere.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.280
 And so I'm going to put it on my desktop,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.560
 and I'm going to call it py4efund.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:56.800
 And so we should see a new file on my database right there.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.760
 Py4efund.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.520
 Now that's a file that you don't want to edit

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.040
 with a text editor or anything like that.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:07.040
 This is a file that's to be read

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.660
 by SQLite browser and nothing else.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.680
 Okay, so we're going to create a table,

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.240
 and I'm going to make a table called users

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.520
 with a column called name that's a text

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.600
 and a column called email.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.440
 So it's already asking me to make a table,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.760
 I'm going to call this users,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.120
 and I'm going to add a field that is called name,

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.380
 and I'm going to add a text,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.800
 and I'm going to add another field called email,

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.600
 and I'm going to make that be text.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.240
 Now the key thing here is we are, in effect,

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.780
 making columns and rendering an opinion

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.520
 as to exactly what that column is supposed to be used for,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.120
 and we're not allowed to violate that.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.120
 It's not like, oh, we'll do whatever you want,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.080
 because the database is optimizing its storage

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.400
 based on a contract that we're,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.240
 we're effectively making the contract ourselves.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.160
 We can make these columns anything we wanted,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.000
 but we're just going to,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.240
 we have to, we're going to contract with ourselves.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.600
 And you can see it's kind of small here,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.080
 you can see there's a create table,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.560
 and that's on the slide,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.120
 and that's the SQL way of doing that.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.320
 This user interface is just helping us write SQL.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.320
 So now I'm going to just say okay,

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.400
 and if you take a look,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.820
 you can see that I now have a table users,

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.040
 and I can look at my database structure,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.000
 table users, and away we go.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.560
 And so, so now that's, that is creating it,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.680
 and like I said, here in the slides is the create statement,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.880
 or on the web page, there's the create statement

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.200
 that could have done it.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.160
 Now, we can insert some data.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:46.160
 Let's add a new record to this database users,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.520
 and we'll call this guy

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.440
 name charlescsev@umich.edu.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.680
 So now we have a record,

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.760
 so it's kind of like a database, a spreadsheet.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.760
 Now, that's not the SQL way to do it,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.040
 there's SQL sort of going on in the background,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.840
 but if we really want to do this using SQL,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.360
 we're going to use the insert statement.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:17.320
 And the insert statement looks like this.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.000
 The SQL syntax sometimes has extra words.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.580
 Insert into is actually two SQL keywords,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.480
 the name of the table, the columns,

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.000
 and then the word values,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.820
 and then one-to-one correspondence

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.840
 between the values and in parentheses,

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.060
 so it looks kind of like a tuple in Python,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.040
 but we're nowhere near Python right now.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.680
 Okay, and so that's what we're going to do,

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:49.320
 and so I'm going to grab this, Kristen,

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.560
 and I'm going to go over here to my SQLite browser

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.700
 and say execute SQL.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.820
 So now I can say paste that in,

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.720
 and then hit this little run button,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.360
 and that's going to submit the SQL to SQLite,

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.200
 and then update that file,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.120
 and it says query executed successfully,

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:09.140
 and away we go.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.080
 So if I go back now and I look at the data,

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.700
 I see that there's two things in here,

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.040
 and now I can actually insert all the rest of these.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.520
 Let's go back to my little bit of stuff here.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:22.440
 Let's put all these other rows in.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.440
 It turns out that if I go into the execute SQL

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.080
 and I want to do more than one command at a time,

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.600
 I can put a semicolon at the end of each one of these things,

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.280
 and then I can run them all at the same time.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.880
 I mean, one after another actually is what's going on here.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.680
 So boom, boom, boom, and now I take a look at the data,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.200
 and look, I've got all those things in there.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.840
 Now, eventually, the thing that's going to generate that SQL

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.560
 is a program, not us.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.960
 We're being the database administrator,

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.160
 so we're sort of doing things manually.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:56.960
 Once things get going, you write programs,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.920
 do that insert over and over and over again in Python,

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.960
 or a web language like PHP, or something like that.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.000
 And so that is the insert.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.640
 Now we can get rid of data.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.640
 And so I'm going to say delete from, that's the keyword,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:15.560
 users is the name of a table, where is a where clause.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.840
 We'll have lots of where clauses in SQL,

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.520
 which is, it's not like an if, in effect,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.880
 the delete is going towards the whole table

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.880
 and being turned on and off by this where clause.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.120
 So delete from users, if you didn't put the where clause on,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.120
 will actually delete all the rows.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.680
 But where Ted equals, email equals ted@umich.edu,

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.160
 well, that one is going to make sure it only applies

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:39.140
 to the rows where that is true.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.840
 So I'm going to go over here in SQL,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.120
 and I'm going to say delete from users

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.400
 where email equals ted@umich.edu,

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.240
 and then I'm going to run it.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.800
 'Cause it's only one, I don't need a semicolon

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:49.640
 at the end of it.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:53.760
 And now if I go back and I look at the data, Ted is gone.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:54.600
 Okay.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:00.140
 Update, so the update says,

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:04.840
 updates keyword, users is the name of the table,

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.360
 set is a keyword, and then this is column equals new value,

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:09.280
 and then a where clause.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:12.000
 Again, this update, if we didn't have a where clause,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.640
 would change every row in the table.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.900
 And so where email equals csev@umich.edu.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:26.080
 I got to change that, because I already got the name

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.360
 to be Charles, so you see the name is already Charles,

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.260
 so I'll just execute here.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:35.560
 Make this be Chuck, so we see it.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.480
 And then I run it, and then you take a look at the data,

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.280
 and it's changed.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:41.400
 That's it.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.440
 That's an update statement.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.940
 We're doing, you're doing great, you're doing great.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:46.780
 And so,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:56.580
 the next thing we're going to do is we're going to take a look

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:57.840
 at how we retrieve data.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.120
 Now this is the select statement, select, star,

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.720
 you have a list of columns, and star means all columns,

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.380
 from is a keyword, and then the name of a table.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:08.440
 So this select star for users is the kind of thing

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.740
 you type all the time.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.960
 As a matter of fact, it's what SQLite browser

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.880
 is doing internally to cause this to happen,

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.540
 but we can do it by hand, by saying,

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.640
 select star from users, and then run it.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.780
 And so then we get a little record set

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.160
 that is those four records that are sitting there.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.420
 We can also throw a where clause on the end of it.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.440
 So we say select star from users,

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.280
 where email equals csev@umich.edu,

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:35.720
 and that, again, the select star from users

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:37.860
 goes at the whole table, and the where clause

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:39.980
 goes at the whole table, and then filters out

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.240
 all of the things except one record.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.160
 So the where clause is send it to the table,

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.420
 but then filter based on whatever,

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:49.760
 and so it only shows us that.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.580
 Okay, we're cruising right along here.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:58.340
 You can also put an order by clause on there.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.080
 So we can say select star from users, order by email,

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:03.660
 so that's a column.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.300
 Select star from users, order by email,

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.780
 and so that order's by email.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:14.940
 Or we can change it to name, and we can say descending.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:19.860
 So that's the name in descending order.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.280
 Sorting and selecting are good things

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:25.200
 that databases are really good at.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.600
 So this is the summary of what I've told you.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:31.740
 I said the databases do create, read, update,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.700
 and delete, CRUD, and we've done all those things,

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:38.060
 except we did create, delete, update, read.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:41.440
 That's what we did, and that's the summary of SQL.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.960
 And so you might be saying, "Why did I take so long

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.260
 "to learn such a simple and elegant and beautiful language?"

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:48.960
 'Cause it's not really exciting.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.000
 It's a extremely simple language that's very predictable,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.420
 and you're like, "That's pretty easy."

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:59.060
 And it turns out that some of you may have been using SQL

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:02.280
 in situations, maybe with Microsoft Access or something,

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:03.500
 where you're actually typing this stuff,

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:05.840
 and you just kind of typed it, and you never realized

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.140
 that you were learning a programming language.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.840
 That's why I like SQL, and that's a very declarative language,

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:12.080
 and it's very straightforward.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.340
 It's much harder to learn, I mean, it's much easier

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.520
 to learn SQL than it is to learn Python,

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.520
 because in Python, you have to figure out how loops work

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.020
 and how iteration variables work,

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:22.860
 and you'll notice there's none of that.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:26.020
 And so, but the key is, is we've only started

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:27.020
 to understand the power.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:30.460
 That's the simple ability to move around and update data

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:34.520
 and read data, randomly using these simple sets of commands.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:38.340
 But up next, we're going to look at how you do this

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.200
 with data models and relationships,

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.620
 and really, multiple tables.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:47.960
 (upbeat rock music)

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.460
 (upbeat rock music)

